CAT = "iiqeIndemnity"; SEG = "IND"
Q = [
("The proximate cause of a loss is its:",
 "Dominant or effective cause", ["First cause in time", "Last cause in time", "Cause the insured names on the form"],
 "Proximate cause means the effective or dominant cause. Neither the first nor the last cause in a chain is necessarily the proximate cause."),
("A peril that a policy would cover but for an exclusion, such as war under a fire policy, is an:",
 "Excepted peril", ["Insured peril", "Uninsured peril", "Proximate peril"],
 "An excepted (excluded) peril is removed from cover by an exclusion. An uninsured peril is neither insured nor excluded."),
("Under a fire policy, firemen's water damages a flat while they put out an accidental fire. The water damage is:",
 "Covered, as fire caused it", ["Not covered, as water is uninsured", "Covered only if the flat burned", "Not covered, as firemen caused it"],
 "An uninsured peril (water) arising directly from an insured peril (fire) is treated as caused by the insured peril, so the loss is covered."),
("A tenant's careless cooking starts a fire that damages the insured flat. Under a fire policy the damage is:",
 "Covered, as fire is insured", ["Not covered, as carelessness caused it", "Covered only if a third party was careless", "Covered at half the loss"],
 "Fire, an insured peril, arising from an uninsured peril such as carelessness is still covered; carelessness is not excluded."),
("A flat is damaged by water used to fight a fire in the flat above. Under the flat's household policy, this water damage is:",
 "Recoverable", ["Not recoverable, no fire in the flat", "Recoverable only from the neighbour", "Recoverable only if the fire was arson"],
 "The proximate cause need not happen on the insured premises. Fire caused the water damage, so the loss is recoverable."),
("A personal accident policy excludes death 'directly or indirectly caused by war'. The effect of 'indirectly' is to:",
 "Exclude war as even a remote cause", ["Make no difference to the exclusion", "Cover all wartime deaths", "Apply only on the battlefield"],
 "'Directly or indirectly' widens an exclusion so that the excepted peril need only be a remote contributing factor, as the courts held for an officer killed in wartime."),
("Which statement about proximate cause is correct?",
 "More than one proximate cause may exist", ["Only the last cause can be proximate", "An uninsured cause defeats every claim", "It applies to marine insurance only"],
 "There may be several proximate causes, for example a dishonest employee and a careless supervisor together causing a theft."),
("Without any insured peril in the chain of events, a loss is:",
 "Not recoverable", ["Recoverable if not excluded", "Recoverable in part", "Recoverable under contribution"],
 "An insured peril must always be involved; otherwise the loss is definitely irrecoverable, even if no exclusion applies."),
("Indemnity in insurance means:",
 "Exact compensation for the loss", ["Payment of the full sum insured", "A benefit fixed in the policy", "Compensation plus a profit margin"],
 "Indemnity is exact financial compensation, no more and no less, putting the insured back in the same financial position as before the loss."),
("Why does the principle of indemnity not normally apply to life insurance?",
 "A life has no exact money value", ["Life insurers never pay claims", "The law bans indemnity for any life cover", "Life policies are always assigned"],
 "Death or injury cannot be measured precisely in money, so life and personal accident policies pay a stated benefit rather than an indemnity."),
("Medical expenses cover included in a travel policy is normally:",
 "Indemnity insurance", ["A benefit policy", "Uninsurable by law", "An agreed value policy"],
 "Unless the policy says otherwise, medical expenses cover reimburses actual costs, so it is subject to indemnity, unlike the death benefit."),
("A property policy usually lets the insurer settle a claim by cash payment, repair, replacement or:",
 "Reinstatement", ["Subrogation", "Contribution", "Abandonment to the insurer"],
 "The four methods of providing indemnity are cash, repair, replacement and reinstatement, meaning restoring the property to its pre-loss condition."),
("A marine or liability policy says nothing about the method of settlement. The insurer must settle a valid claim by:",
 "Cash payment", ["Repair", "Replacement", "Reinstatement"],
 "Only property policies usually give the insurer the option of repair, replacement or reinstatement; otherwise the insurer must pay cash."),
("After a total loss the insurer pays in full and takes the damaged stock to sell. The value it recovers is called:",
 "Salvage", ["Abandonment premium", "Contribution", "An excess"],
 "Salvage is the remains of damaged property. Its value is either deducted from the claim or kept by the insurer that pays in full."),
("A shipowner surrenders a wrecked ship to the insurer in return for a total loss payment. This is:",
 "Abandonment", ["Subrogation", "Contribution", "Average"],
 "Abandonment, mostly found in marine insurance, hands the subject matter to the insurer, who may then benefit from its residual value."),
("A shop worth HK$4 million is insured for HK$1 million under a policy subject to average. A HK$200,000 loss is paid at:",
 "HK$50,000", ["HK$200,000", "HK$100,000", "HK$25,000"],
 "The shop is 25% insured (1 of 4 million), so average pays 25% of the HK$200,000 loss, which is HK$50,000."),
("A motor policy has a HK$5,000 excess. The repair bill is HK$18,000. The insurer pays:",
 "HK$13,000", ["HK$18,000", "HK$23,000", "HK$5,000"],
 "An excess is deducted from every claim: HK$18,000 minus HK$5,000 leaves HK$13,000 payable."),
("A hospital policy has a 3-day time franchise. The insured stays in hospital for 6 days. The policy pays expenses for:",
 "All 6 days", ["3 days", "No days at all", "The last 3 days"],
 "Unlike an excess, once a franchise is reached the loss is paid in full, so all 6 days are covered. A stay under 3 days would get nothing."),
("A contents policy pays at most a set amount for any one valuable item that was not separately insured. This is a:",
 "Single article limit", ["Section limit", "Franchise", "Condition of average"],
 "A single article limit caps the payment for one unlisted item; the insured can avoid it by declaring the item with its own sum insured."),
("Which policy provision gives more than a strict indemnity?",
 "New for old cover", ["Condition of average", "Policy excess", "Single article limit"],
 "New for old, reinstatement basis and agreed value cover pay without deductions for wear and tear, giving more than strict indemnity. The others give less."),
]
